From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 17:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] DwUsbHostDxe: optimize the transfer hot path

Three costs sat on the path every USB transaction takes, and all three
are paid at TPL_NOTIFY, where nothing else on the system can run.

Wait4Chhltd opened with MicroSecondDelay (100) before waiting for the
channel to halt, and ran another after the wait before reading HCINT.
Neither is necessary. HCINT is written by the same hardware event that
raises CHHLTD, so the bits are already valid once Wait4Bit returns. The
reference driver does not delay on either side: u-boot's
wait_for_chhltd calls wait_for_bit_le32 on CHHLTD and reads HCINT
immediately after, and the only udelay/mdelay calls in that driver are
in core reset and port power. 200 us on every transaction is a fifth of
each tick on an endpoint polled once a millisecond by the periodic
handler, spent in a busy wait that nothing below TPL_NOTIFY can
interrupt.

Wait4Bit called gBS->CheckEvent once per register read. CheckEvent is a
boot service that raises to TPL_HIGH_LEVEL, walks the event and
restores; it costs far more than the MmioRead32 it was guarding, so the
deadline rather than the hardware set the pace of the loop. Check it
once every DWC2_POLLS_PER_TIMEOUT_CHECK reads instead. A millisecond
timeout is still noticed within microseconds of expiry, and the loop now
converges at the speed the controller can actually answer.

DwHcDeferredTransfer created a timeout event, armed it, and closed it on
every call -- a pool allocation, an event list insertion and a matching
teardown, a thousand times a second for each armed endpoint. Deferred
transfers only ever run from DwHcPeriodicHandler at TPL_NOTIFY, one at a
time, and nothing below TPL_NOTIFY can preempt them, so they cannot
overlap and one event per controller suffices. Create it in
CreateDwUsbHc, re-arm it per tick, close it in DestroyDwUsbHc.

That last change needs care that the old code got for free by throwing
the event away each time. TimerForTransfer is TimerRelative, so a
transfer that finishes early leaves the timer armed; if it then fires,
the event carries a signal into the next tick and the first Wait4Bit
there reports a timeout immediately, cutting a healthy transfer short.
Cancel the timer and drain the event on the way out.

No functional change is intended beyond the timing.

Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
@@ -47,6 +47,12 @@
 #define TimerForTransfer TimerRelative
 
 /*
+ * How many register reads Wait4Bit performs between consulting its timeout
+ * event. See the comment there.
+ */
+#define DWC2_POLLS_PER_TIMEOUT_CHECK  64
+
+/*
  * https://www.quicklogic.com/assets/pdf/data-sheets/QL-Hi-Speed-USB-2.0-OTG-Controller-Data-Sheet.pdf
  */
 
@@ -87,8 +93,9 @@
   )
 {
   UINT32 Value;
+  UINTN  PollsLeft = DWC2_POLLS_PER_TIMEOUT_CHECK;
 
-  do {
+  for (;;) {
     Value = MmioRead32 (Reg);
     if (!Set) {
       Value = ~Value;
@@ -97,9 +104,23 @@
     if ((Value & Mask) == Mask) {
       return EFI_SUCCESS;
     }
-  } while (EFI_ERROR (gBS->CheckEvent (Timeout)));
 
-  return EFI_TIMEOUT;
+    /*
+     * gBS->CheckEvent is a boot service: it raises to TPL_HIGH_LEVEL, walks
+     * the event and restores, which costs far more than the single register
+     * read it is guarding. Consulting it after every read let the deadline
+     * rather than the hardware set the pace of this loop, on a path that
+     * runs for every USB transaction the controller performs. Checking once
+     * per DWC2_POLLS_PER_TIMEOUT_CHECK reads still notices a millisecond
+     * timeout within microseconds of its expiry.
+     */
+    if (--PollsLeft == 0) {
+      PollsLeft = DWC2_POLLS_PER_TIMEOUT_CHECK;
+      if (!EFI_ERROR (gBS->CheckEvent (Timeout))) {
+        return EFI_TIMEOUT;
+      }
+    }
+  }
 }
 
 CHANNEL_HALT_REASON
@@ -117,14 +138,26 @@
   UINT32  Hcint, Hctsiz;
   UINT32  HcintCompHltAck = DWC2_HCINT_XFERCOMP;
 
-  MicroSecondDelay (100);
+  /*
+   * There were two unconditional MicroSecondDelay (100) calls here, one
+   * before waiting for the channel to halt and one before reading the
+   * interrupt status afterwards. Neither is needed: HCINT is written by
+   * the same hardware event that raises CHHLTD, so once Wait4Bit has seen
+   * CHHLTD the remaining bits are already valid. The reference driver
+   * agrees -- u-boot's wait_for_chhltd waits on CHHLTD and reads HCINT
+   * straight after, with no delay on either side.
+   *
+   * They were not free. 200 us went by on every transaction the controller
+   * ran, which on an endpoint polled once a millisecond from the
+   * TPL_NOTIFY periodic handler is a fifth of every tick spent in a busy
+   * wait that nothing below TPL_NOTIFY can interrupt.
+   */
   Status = Wait4Bit (Timeout, DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCINT (Channel),
                      DWC2_HCINT_CHHLTD, 1);
   if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
     return XFER_NOT_HALTED;
   }
 
-  MicroSecondDelay (100);
   Hcint = MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCINT (Channel));
 
   ASSERT ((Hcint & DWC2_HCINT_CHHLTD) != 0);
@@ -468,19 +501,21 @@
   )
 {
   EFI_STATUS Status;
-  EFI_EVENT TimeoutEvt = NULL;
-
-  Status = gBS->CreateEvent (EVT_TIMER, 0, NULL, NULL, &TimeoutEvt);
-  ASSERT_EFI_ERROR (Status);
-  if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
-    goto Exit;
-  }
+  EFI_EVENT  TimeoutEvt = Req->DwHc->DeferredTimeoutEvent;
 
+  /*
+   * This runs from DwHcPeriodicHandler at TPL_NOTIFY, once a millisecond
+   * for every armed endpoint, and nothing below TPL_NOTIFY can preempt it
+   * -- so a single timeout event per controller can be re-armed here
+   * rather than one being created and destroyed on every tick. The pool
+   * allocation, event list insertion and matching teardown that cost paid
+   * for were happening a thousand times a second per endpoint.
+   */
   Status = gBS->SetTimer (TimeoutEvt, TimerForTransfer,
                   EFI_TIMER_PERIOD_MILLISECONDS (Req->TimeOut));
   ASSERT_EFI_ERROR (Status);
   if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
-    goto Exit;
+    return;
   }
 
   Req->TransferResult = EFI_USB_NOERROR;
@@ -505,9 +540,15 @@
          Req->CallbackContext,
          Req->TransferResult);
 Exit:
-  if (TimeoutEvt != NULL) {
-    gBS->CloseEvent (TimeoutEvt);
-  }
+  /*
+   * TimerForTransfer is TimerRelative, so a transfer that completed early
+   * leaves the timer still armed. Cancel it, then drain any signal it has
+   * already raised: this event outlives the call now, and a stale signal
+   * carried into the next tick would make the first Wait4Bit there report
+   * a timeout at once and cut a healthy transfer short.
+   */
+  gBS->SetTimer (TimeoutEvt, TimerCancel, 0);
+  gBS->CheckEvent (TimeoutEvt);
 }
 
 /**
@@ -1783,6 +1824,10 @@
     gBS->RestoreTPL (PreviousTpl);
   }
 
+  if (DwHc->DeferredTimeoutEvent != NULL) {
+    gBS->CloseEvent (DwHc->DeferredTimeoutEvent);
+  }
+
   if (DwHc->ExitBootServiceEvent != NULL) {
     gBS->CloseEvent (DwHc->ExitBootServiceEvent);
   }
@@ -1949,6 +1994,13 @@
     return Status;
   }
 
+  Status = gBS->CreateEvent (EVT_TIMER, 0, NULL, NULL,
+                  &DwHc->DeferredTimeoutEvent);
+  if (Status != EFI_SUCCESS) {
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "CreateDwUsbHc: DeferredTimeoutEvent: %r\n", Status));
+    return Status;
+  }
+
   Status = gBS->CreateEvent (
                   EVT_TIMER | EVT_NOTIFY_SIGNAL,
                   TPL_NOTIFY,
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.h
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.h
@@ -105,6 +105,13 @@
 
   EFI_EVENT                       PeriodicEvent;
 
+  /*
+   * Re-armed by DwHcDeferredTransfer on every tick. One per controller is
+   * enough: deferred transfers only ever run from the TPL_NOTIFY periodic
+   * handler, one at a time, so they cannot overlap.
+   */
+  EFI_EVENT                       DeferredTimeoutEvent;
+
   EFI_PHYSICAL_ADDRESS            DwUsbBase;
   UINT8                           *StatusBuffer;
 
